"text": "Is your Windows PC slow to start, slow to open apps, or freezing for no reason?\nIn this video, I'll show you ten fixes on a real Windows PC.\nEvery fix is free, and none of them deletes your files.\nMost of them take less than a minute.\nAnd stay to the end, because fix number seven frees up space without deleting anything you need.\nLet's start with the one that makes the biggest difference.\nMany apps start by themselves every time you turn on your PC.\nEach one makes startup slower and keeps using memory in the background.\nPress Control, Shift, Escape to open Task Manager.\nOn the left, click Startup apps.\nHere you can see every app that starts with Windows, and its startup impact.\nThis one is called HeavyApp, and it starts every time.\nSelect it, then click Disable at the top.\nThe status now says Disabled.\nThe app still works, it just won't start by itself anymore.\nDo this for every app you don't need right away, like chat apps, updaters and game launchers.\nIf your PC is slow right now, something is using your processor or memory.\nOpen Task Manager again, and stay on Processes.\nClick the CPU column to sort by what is working hardest.\nLook at the app at the top.\nHere, HeavyApp is using almost half of the processor.\nClick Memory to check that column the same way.\nIf you don't need the app, save your work, select it and click End task.\nLook at the CPU total now, it dropped right down.\nSometimes the taskbar, the Start menu or File Explorer freezes.\nYou don't need to restart the whole computer.\nIn Task Manager, find Windows Explorer in the list.\nSelect it, then click Restart task at the top.\nThe taskbar disappears for a second and comes back fresh.\nYour open apps stay open.\nHere is a hidden setting that makes frozen apps easy to close.\nOpen Settings, then System, then For developers.\nTurn on End task.\nNow right-click any app on your taskbar.\nYou get a new option called End task.\nClick it, and the app closes right away, even if it's frozen.\nNo more opening Task Manager every time.\nIf these fixes are helping, subscribe to TM Diamond for more Windows tutorials like this.\nNow, the next two fixes make Windows feel faster straight away.\nWindows uses animations and see-through effects on menus and windows.\nThey look nice, but they use your graphics and processor.\nOpen Settings, then Accessibility, then Visual effects.\nTurn off Animation effects.\nThen turn off Transparency effects.\nMenus and windows now open without the fade and slide.\nThere is an older setting that goes even further.\nSearch for performance in the Start menu, and open Adjust the appearance and performance of Windows.\nIn this window, select Adjust for best performance.\nThis turns off the extra visual effects all at once.\nClick OK to apply it.\nWindows looks a little plainer, but it responds faster on older PCs.\nNow the fix I promised.\nWindows keeps temporary files that you don't need anymore.\nOpen Settings, then System, then Storage.\nClick Temporary files.\nWindows shows how much space each kind of file uses.\nOn this PC, the selected files add up to four hundred and seventeen megabytes.\nTick the ones you don't need, then click Remove files.\nClick Continue to confirm.\nBe careful with the Downloads folder, because it can contain files you still want.\nYou don't have to clean up by hand every time.\nGo back to Storage, and open Storage Sense.\nTurn on automatic user content cleanup.\nNow Windows deletes temporary files for you.\nYou can also choose how often it runs, for example every week, or when disk space is low.\nOld apps take up space, and some of them run in the background.\nOpen Settings, then Apps, then Installed apps.\nSet Sort by to Size, large to small, so the biggest apps are at the top.\nClick the three dots next to an app you don't use.\nThen choose Uninstall.\nOnly remove apps you recognize and don't need.\nThe last fix keeps your drive running smoothly.\nSearch for defragment, and open Defragment and Optimize Drives.\nSelect your C drive.\nClick Optimize.\nOn a hard drive, Windows rearranges the files so they load faster.\nOn an SSD, Windows sends a trim command instead, which keeps it fast.\nWindows normally does this every week, but it's worth checking it is turned on.\nThose are ten free fixes for a slow Windows PC.\nStart with startup apps and temporary files, they make the biggest difference.\nNext, watch my video on fifteen Command Prompt commands every Windows user should know.\nFollow TM Diamond for more step-by-step Windows tutorials."
